Repair for the LocalSet lost wakeup that the "localset" configuration reaches.

`Shared::schedule` has three arms; two push the task and then call
`self.waker.wake()`. The first arm is the only one that pushes with no
notification, justified by the comment "If the current `LocalSet` is being
polled, we don't need to wake it" -- i.e. by the assumption that the poll in
flight will `tick()` the queue afterwards.

`RunUntil::poll` breaks that assumption: it registers its waker with the set,
polls the inner future, and on `Poll::Ready` returns immediately, skipping both
the `tick()` that would drain the queue and the wake that would notify. A task
woken during that final poll is left queued with nothing scheduled to look at
it. `LocalSet::poll` has no such early return.

The repair discharges the deferred obligation at the point that skipped it. It
does not change when tasks run; it restores only the omitted notification.

Applies to the extracted tokio 1.52.3 crate (`git apply -p1` from its root).
`RunUntil::poll` and `Shared::schedule` are byte-identical in 1.52.3 and in
1.53.1 / master 108d6d3, where this repair was verified against the full
upstream test suite (173 suites, 1364 tests clean). Generated mechanically from
the published 1.52.3 source.

--- a/src/task/local.rs
+++ b/src/task/local.rs
@@ -1070,6 +1070,16 @@
             let f = me.future;
 
             if let Poll::Ready(output) = f.poll(cx) {
+                // The poll above may have woken tasks belonging to this
+                // `LocalSet`. Because we are inside the guard scope opened
+                // above, those wakes took the silent arm of `Shared::schedule`,
+                // which pushes with no notification on the assumption that the
+                // poll in flight will `tick()` afterwards. We are returning
+                // instead of ticking, so discharge that assumption here: wake
+                // the set's registered waker so a remaining driver drains the
+                // queue. With no other driver this is a no-op beyond one
+                // spurious wake of the task that is completing.
+                me.local_set.context.shared.waker.wake();
                 return Poll::Ready(output);
             }
 
